<h1>Third-party licences</h1>
<p>sc-watch is built from these open-source libraries. Their licences follow,
after the components that are not Rust libraries.</p>
<h2 id="iris">IRIS - nameplate finder, letter reader, mining reader and their data</h2>
<p>From <a href="https://github.com/Niteb1rd/IRIS">IRIS</a> by Niteb1rd, under
the GNU General Public License v3.0 - the same licence as sc-watch, whose full
text is <code>LICENSE.txt</code> in the sc-watch folder.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Taken from IRIS 2.0.0 (commit aec86f08), with three detection fixes from
  its pull request #1 (commit 29c3250e95), and the line join (BandJoin) of
  IRIS 2.1.0.</li>
  <li>Finding nameplates and reading them, ported from C# to Rust (by way of
  sc-watch's Python port) in the scw-vision library. None of IRIS's player
  tracking, network or lookup code was taken.</li>
  <li>The mining overlay: IRIS's mining signal reader, its steadying of reads
  over frames and the label's layout, ported from C# to Rust (scw-vision's
  mining module, scw-overlay). Its digit shapes (iris-mining-digits.txt) and
  its table of rock signals (iris-mining.txt) are IRIS's, unchanged.</li>
  <li>The letter model (iris-letters-model.bin), the digit shapes and the
  table are built into the sc-watch and sc-watch-watcher programs.</li>
  <li>The list of labels that are not players is taken in part from IRIS's
  exclusion list.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="ppocr">PP-OCRv3 - the OCR models, and RapidOCR's steps around them</h2>
<p>Chat reads, and the legacy reader for players who keep the game's own HDR
option on, use PaddleOCR's PP-OCRv3 text detector and recogniser
(<a href="https://github.com/PaddlePaddle/PaddleOCR">PaddleOCR</a>, Copyright
(c) 2020 PaddlePaddle Authors) as
<a href="https://github.com/RapidAI/RapidOCR">RapidOCR</a> 1.2.3 ships them -
both under the Apache License 2.0, whose text follows this list.</p>
<ul>
  <li>The two models (ch_PP-OCRv3_det_infer.onnx, ch_PP-OCRv3_rec_infer.onnx)
  and the recogniser's character list, unchanged, are built into the sc-watch
  and sc-watch-watcher programs, and run by rten.</li>
  <li>RapidOCR's steps around them - the detector's sizing and
  post-processing, the cut-out lines, the recogniser's batches and decoding -
  are ported from Python to Rust in the scw-ocr library.</li>
  <li>Parts of <a href="https://opencv.org">OpenCV</a> 5.0 (Apache License
  2.0) are reimplemented in Rust to give its results, for the OCR and for
  IRIS: contour tracing, convex hull, minimum-area rectangle, polygon fill,
  perspective transform and warp, resizing, colour conversion, morphology
  and connected components.</li>
  <li>The detector's box growing follows Clipper 6.4.2's polygon offsetting
  by Angus Johnson (Boost Software License 1.0), as pyclipper wraps it.</li>
</ul>
